 The **Auto failover** tab orders a chain of entries: each Claude account, each API profile, and each provider without an account of its own here (Copilot through `gh`, a key in the settings, Ollama, an agent CLI). A task stopped by a rate limit or a lost connection resumes on the next usable entry, through the same path as « Resume with… », at the phase where it stopped; the task's log says from where to where it moved. An entry already tried for that task is skipped for 6 hours, an entry you removed from the chain keeps its place, and a task moves at most 6 times. Design: [provider-failover.md](https://github.com/krovomi/WorkPilot-AI/blob/develop/docs/architecture/provider-failover.md).
 
+**Several accounts per provider.** A provider with an API key (OpenAI, Google, Mistral, DeepSeek, Grok, Meta, OpenRouter, Hugging Face, Cursor, Windsurf; AWS as a key pair and region) can hold several keys, encrypted in the app's main process, one of them the default given to tasks; Claude keeps its OAuth profiles, and Copilot, Codex CLI and Gemini CLI get one sign-in folder per account. The provider popover switches the default, each account shows its spend against an optional monthly budget, and a project or a single Kanban card can pin its own account. When a task hits a 401, a 429 or a spent quota, failover first tries the other accounts of the same provider, then the rest of the chain; only the account changes. On a team server, each member keeps their own accounts there. Design: [ai-accounts.md](https://github.com/krovomi/WorkPilot-AI/blob/develop/docs/architecture/ai-accounts.md).
+
 **Plan names.** The usage breakdown shows each account's plan as the provider reports it: Claude Pro, Max 5x or Max 20x (read from Claude Code's sign-in), ChatGPT Plus or Pro (Codex CLI), Copilot Free, Pro, Pro+, Business or Enterprise, Windsurf, Cursor. An account billed by API key says « API key · pay as you go »; a provider that does not name its plan shows « Subscription ». Nothing is guessed and no extra network call is made.
 
+## Model gateways: OpenRouter and Hugging Face
+
+OpenRouter and Hugging Face Inference Providers are providers of their own, driven by the OpenAI-compatible client like Mistral, DeepSeek and Grok: one key (`OPENROUTER_API_KEY`, `HUGGINGFACE_API_KEY` or `HF_TOKEN`), hundreds of models. The model list is read live, without a key, and keeps only the models that call tools; price and context window come from the gateway, effort goes through `reasoning.effort`, and the key is tested on the account, never on the list. OpenRouter adds fallbacks, a provider order and a privacy setting; a Hub model can be tried in the cloud before it is picked. GitHub issue triage can compare issues by meaning with Hugging Face embeddings. Design: [gateway-providers.md](https://github.com/krovomi/WorkPilot-AI/blob/develop/docs/architecture/gateway-providers.md).
